{"type":"user","uuid":"402d757c-e32d-4b1f-aad2-3fafbcbce1a5","session_id":"b6db9417-93e9-42c0-ae53-1ecc09abc1e9","parent_tool_use_id":null,"message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call_0fcea7600b084e2982d53de1","is_error":false,"content":"package fr.neamar.kiss.forwarder;\n\nimport android.accessibilityservice.AccessibilityServiceInfo;\nimport android.annotation.SuppressLint;\nimport android.app.AlertDialog;\nimport android.content.Context;\nimport android.content.Intent;\nimport android.content.pm.ActivityInfo;\nimport android.content.pm.ServiceInfo;\nimport android.os.Build;\nimport android.os.Handler;\nimport android.provider.Settings;\nimport android.text.InputType;\nimport android.view.GestureDetector;\nimport android.view.MotionEvent;\nimport android.view.View;\nimport android.view.accessibility.AccessibilityManager;\nimport android.widget.ImageView;\n\nimport java.lang.reflect.InvocationTargetException;\nimport java.lang.reflect.Method;\nimport java.util.List;\n\nimport fr.neamar.kiss.MainActivity;\nimport fr.neamar.kiss.R;\nimport fr.neamar.kiss.searcher.HistorySearcher;\nimport fr.neamar.kiss.searcher.NullSearcher;\nimport fr.neamar.kiss.searcher.Searcher;\nimport fr.neamar.kiss.utils.LockAccessibilityService;\n\n// Deals with any settings in the \"User Experience\" setting sub-screen\nclass ExperienceTweaks extends Forwarder {\n    /**\n     * InputType that behaves as if the consuming IME is a standard-obeying\n     * soft-keyboard\n     * <p>\n     * *Auto Complete* means \"we're handling auto-completion ourselves\". Then\n     * we ignore whatever the IME thinks we should display.\n     */\n    private final static int INPUT_TYPE_STANDARD = InputType.TYPE_CLASS_TEXT\n            | InputType.TYPE_TEXT_FLAG_AUTO_COMPLETE\n            | InputType.TYPE_TEXT_FLAG_NO_SUGGESTIONS;\n    /**\n     * InputType that behaves as if the consuming IME is SwiftKey\n     * <p>\n     * *Visible Password* fields will break many non-Latin IMEs and may show\n     * unexpected behaviour in numerous ways. (#454, #517)\n     */\n    private final static int INPUT_TYPE_WORKAROUND = InputType.TYPE_TEXT_VARIATION_VISIBLE_PASSWORD\n            | InputType.TYPE_TEXT_FLAG_AUTO_CORRECT;\n\n    private final Runnable displayKeyboardRunnable = mainActivity::showKeyboard;\n\n    private View mainEmptyView;\n    private final GestureDetector gd;\n\n    @SuppressLint(\"SourceLockedOrientationActivity\")\n    ExperienceTweaks(final MainActivity mainActivity) {\n        super(mainActivity);\n\n        // Lock launcher into portrait mode\n        // Do it here (before initializing the view in onCreate) to make the transition as smooth as possible\n        if (prefs.getBoolean(\"force-portrait\", true)) {\n            if (Build.VERSION.SDK_INT >= Build.VERSION_CODES.JELLY_BEAN_MR2) {\n                mainActivity.setRequestedOrientation(ActivityInfo.SCREEN_ORIENTATION_USER_PORTRAIT);\n            } else {\n                mainActivity.setRequestedOrientation(ActivityInfo.SCREEN_ORIENTATION_PORTRAIT);\n            }\n        } else {\n            mainActivity.setRequestedOrientation(ActivityInfo.SCREEN_ORIENTATION_USER);\n        }\n\n        gd = new GestureDetector(mainActivity, new GestureDetector.SimpleOnGestureListener() {\n            @Override\n            public boolean onSingleTapUp(MotionEvent e) {\n                // Double tap disabled: display history directly\n                if(!prefs.getBoolean(\"double-tap\", false)) {\n                    if (prefs.getBoolean(\"history-onclick\", false)) {\n                        doAction(\"display-history\");\n                    }\n                }\n                return super.onSingleTapUp(e);\n            }\n\n            @Override\n            public boolean onSingleTapConfirmed(MotionEvent e) {\n                // Double tap enabled: wait to confirm this is indeed a single tap, not a double tap\n                if(prefs.getBoolean(\"double-tap\", false)) {\n                    if (prefs.getBoolean(\"history-onclick\", false)) {\n                        doAction(\"display-history\");\n                    }\n                }\n\n                return super.onSingleTapConfirmed(e);\n            }\n\n            @Override\n            public boolean onDoubleTap(MotionEvent e) {\n                if (Build.VERSION.SDK_INT < Build.VERSION_CODES.P) {\n                    return super.onDoubleTap(e);\n                }\n                if(!prefs.getBoolean(\"double-tap\", false)) {\n                    return super.onDoubleTap(e);\n                }\n\n                if (isAccessibilityServiceEnabled(mainActivity)) {\n                    Intent intent = new Intent(LockAccessibilityService.ACTION_LOCK, null, mainActivity, LockAccessibilityService.class);\n                    mainActivity.startService(intent);\n                } else {\n                    AlertDialog.Builder builder = new AlertDialog.Builder(mainActivity);\n                    builder.setMessage(R.string.enable_double_tap_to_lock);\n\n                    builder.setPositiveButton(android.R.string.ok, (dialog, which) -> {\n                        Intent intent = new Intent(Settings.ACTION_ACCESSIBILITY_SETTINGS);\n                        intent.addFlags(Intent.FLAG_ACTIVITY_NEW_TASK);\n                        mainActivity.startActivity(intent);\n                    });\n\n                    builder.setNegativeButton(android.R.string.cancel, (dialog, which) -> {\n                        dialog.dismiss();\n                    });\n\n                    AlertDialog alert = builder.create();\n                    alert.show();\n                }\n                return super.onDoubleTap(e);\n            }\n\n            @Override\n            public boolean onFling(MotionEvent e1, MotionEvent e2, float velocityX, float velocityY) {\n                float directionY = e2.getY() - e1.getY();\n                float directionX = e2.getX() - e1.getX();\n                if (Math.abs(directionX) > Math.abs(directionY)) {\n                    return false;\n                }\n                if (directionY > 0) {\n                    doAction(prefs.getString(\"gesture-down\", \"display-notifications\"));\n                } else {\n                    doAction(prefs.getString(\"gesture-up\", \"display-keyboard\"));\n                }\n                return true;\n            }\n\n            private void doAction(String action) {\n                switch (action) {\n                    case \"display-notifications\":\n                        displayNotificationDrawer();\n                        break;\n                    case \"display-keyboard\":\n                        mainActivity.showKeyboard();\n                        break;\n                    case \"display-apps\":\n                        if (mainActivity.isViewingSearchResults()) {\n                            mainActivity.displayKissBar(true);\n                        }\n                        break;\n                    case \"display-history\":\n                        // if minimalistic mode is enabled,\n                        if (isMinimalisticModeEnabled()) {\n                            // and we're currently in minimalistic mode with no results,\n                            // and we're not looking at the app list\n                            if (mainActivity.isViewingSearchResults() && mainActivity.searchEditText.getText().toString().isEmpty()) {\n                                if (mainActivity.list.getAdapter() == null || mainActivity.list.getAdapter().isEmpty()) {\n                                    mainActivity.runTask(new HistorySearcher(mainActivity));\n                                    mainActivity.clearButton.setVisibility(View.VISIBLE);\n                                    mainActivity.menuButton.setVisibility(View.INVISIBLE);\n                                }\n                            }\n                        }\n\n                        if (isMinimalisticModeEnabledForFavorites()) {\n                            mainActivity.favoritesBar.setVisibility(View.VISIBLE);\n                        }\n                        break;\n                }\n            }\n        });\n    }\n\n    void onCreate() {\n        mainEmptyView = mainActivity.findViewById(R.id.main_empty);\n    }\n\n    void onResume() {\n        adjustInputType();\n\n        // Activity manifest specifies stateAlwaysHidden as windowSoftInputMode\n        // so the keyboard will be hidden by default\n        // we may want to display it if the setting is set\n        if (isKeyboardOnStartEnabled()) {\n            // Display keyboard\n            mainActivity.showKeyboard();\n\n            new Handler().postDelayed(displayKeyboardRunnable, 10);\n            // For some weird reasons, keyboard may be hidden by the system\n            // So we have to run this multiple time at different time\n            // See https://github.com/Neamar/KISS/issues/119\n            new Handler().postDelayed(displayKeyboardRunnable, 100);\n            new Handler().postDelayed(displayKeyboardRunnable, 500);\n        } else {\n            // Not used (thanks windowSoftInputMode)\n            // unless coming back from KISS settings\n            mainActivity.hideKeyboard();\n        }\n\n        if (isMinimalisticModeEnabled()) {\n            mainEmptyView.setVisibility(View.GONE);\n\n            mainActivity.list.setVerticalScrollBarEnabled(false);\n            mainActivity.searchEditText.setHint(\"\");\n        }\n        if (prefs.getBoolean(\"pref-hide-circle\", false)) {\n            ((ImageView) mainActivity.launcherButton).setImageBitmap(null);\n            ((ImageView) mainActivity.menuButton).setImageBitmap(null);\n        }\n    }\n\n    void onTouch(MotionEvent event) {\n        // Forward touch events to the gesture detector\n        gd.onTouchEvent(event);\n    }\n\n    void onWindowFocusChanged(boolean hasFocus) {\n        if (hasFocus && isKeyboardOnStartEnabled()) {\n            mainActivity.showKeyboard();\n        }\n    }\n\n    void onDisplayKissBar(boolean display) {\n        if (isMinimalisticModeEnabledForFavorites() && !display) {\n            mainActivity.favoritesBar.setVisibility(View.GONE);\n        }\n\n        if (!display && isKeyboardOnStartEnabled()) {\n            // Display keyboard\n            mainActivity.showKeyboard();\n        }\n    }\n\n    void updateSearchRecords(boolean isRefresh, String query) {\n        if (query.isEmpty()) {\n            if (isMinimalisticModeEnabled()) {\n                mainActivity.runTask(new NullSearcher(mainActivity));\n                // By default, help text is displayed -- not in minimalistic mode.\n                mainEmptyView.setVisibility(View.GONE);\n\n                if (isMinimalisticModeEnabledForFavorites()) {\n                    mainActivity.favoritesBar.setVisibility(View.GONE);\n                }\n            } else {\n                Searcher searcher = new HistorySearcher(mainActivity);\n                searcher.setRefresh(isRefresh);\n                mainActivity.runTask(searcher);\n            }\n        }\n    }\n\n    // Ensure the keyboard uses the right input method\n    private void adjustInputType() {\n        int currentInputType = mainActivity.searchEditText.getInputType();\n        int requiredInputType;\n\n        if (isSuggestionsEnabled()) {\n            requiredInputType = InputType.TYPE_CLASS_TEXT;\n        } else {\n            if (isNonCompliantKeyboard()) {\n                requiredInputType = INPUT_TYPE_WORKAROUND;\n            } else {\n                requiredInputType = INPUT_TYPE_STANDARD;\n            }\n        }\n        if (currentInputType != requiredInputType) {\n            mainActivity.searchEditText.setInputType(requiredInputType);\n        }\n    }\n\n    // Super hacky code to display notification drawer\n    // Can (and will) break in any Android release.\n    @SuppressLint(\"PrivateApi\")\n    @SuppressWarnings(\"CatchAndPrintStackTrace\")\n    private void displayNotificationDrawer() {\n        @SuppressLint(\"WrongConstant\") Object sbservice = mainActivity.getSystemService(\"statusbar\");\n        Class<?> statusbarManager;\n        try {\n            statusbarManager = Class.forName(\"android.app.StatusBarManager\");\n            Method showStatusBar;\n            if (Build.VERSION.SDK_INT >= 17) {\n                showStatusBar = statusbarManager.getMethod(\"expandNotificationsPanel\");\n            } else {\n                showStatusBar = statusbarManager.getMethod(\"expand\");\n            }\n            showStatusBar.invoke(sbservice);\n        } catch (ClassNotFoundException e1) {\n            e1.printStackTrace();\n        } catch (NoSuchMethodException e1) {\n            e1.printStackTrace();\n        } catch (IllegalAccessException e1) {\n            e1.printStackTrace();\n        } catch (InvocationTargetException e1) {\n            e1.printStackTrace();\n        }\n    }\n\n    private boolean isMinimalisticModeEnabled() {\n        return prefs.getBoolean(\"history-hide\", false);\n    }\n\n    private boolean isMinimalisticModeEnabledForFavorites() {\n        return prefs.getBoolean(\"history-hide\", false) && prefs.getBoolean(\"favorites-hide\", false) && prefs.getBoolean(\"enable-favorites-bar\", true);\n    }\n\n    /**\n     * Should we force the keyboard not to display suggestions?\n     * (swiftkey is broken, see https://github.com/Neamar/KISS/issues/44)\n     * (same for flesky: https://github.com/Neamar/KISS/issues/1263)\n     */\n    private boolean isNonCompliantKeyboard() {\n        String currentKeyboard = Settings.Secure.getString(mainActivity.getContentResolver(), Settings.Secure.DEFAULT_INPUT_METHOD).toLowerCase();\n        return currentKeyboard.contains(\"swiftkey\") || currentKeyboard.contains(\"flesky\");\n    }\n\n    /**\n     * Should the keyboard be displayed by default?\n     */\n    private boolean isKeyboardOnStartEnabled() {\n        return prefs.getBoolean(\"display-keyboard\", false);\n    }\n\n    /**\n     * Should the keyboard autocomplete and suggest options\n     */\n    private boolean isSuggestionsEnabled() {\n        return prefs.getBoolean(\"enable-suggestions-keyboard\", false);\n    }\n\n    /**\n     * Are we allowed to run our AccessibilityService?\n     */\n    private boolean isAccessibilityServiceEnabled(Context context) {\n        AccessibilityManager am = (AccessibilityManager) context.getSystemService(Context.ACCESSIBILITY_SERVICE);\n        if (am == null) {\n            return false;\n        }\n\n        List<AccessibilityServiceInfo> enabledServices = am.getEnabledAccessibilityServiceList(AccessibilityServiceInfo.FEEDBACK_ALL_MASK);\n\n\n        for (AccessibilityServiceInfo enabledService : enabledServices) {\n            ServiceInfo enabledServiceInfo = enabledService.getResolveInfo().serviceInfo;\n            if (enabledServiceInfo.packageName.equals(context.getPackageName()) && enabledServiceInfo.name.equals(LockAccessibilityService.class.getName()))\n                return true;\n        }\n\n        return false;\n    }\n}\n"}]}}
{"type":"assistant","uuid":"74b96da7-468c-4233-a3a9-8859e650a6ca","session_id":"b6db9417-93e9-42c0-ae53-1ecc09abc1e9","parent_tool_use_id":null,"message":{"id":"74b96da7-468c-4233-a3a9-8859e650a6ca","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"qwen/qwen3.6-35b-a3b","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I understand the bug. Let me trace through the issue:\n\n1. When the user taps on the screen, the `GestureDetector` in `ExperienceTweaks` processes the touch event.\n2. For a single tap, if `history-onclick` is enabled, it calls `doAction(\"display-history\")`.\n3. In the `doAction(\"display-history\")` case, the favorites bar is shown with `mainActivity.favoritesBar.setVisibility(View.VISIBLE)` — **but only** when `isMinimalisticModeEnabledForFavorites()` is true.\n\nThe problem is more subtle. Let me re-read the bug description:\n\n> 1. Set an app as a favorite.\n> 2. Enable \"Show favorites above search bar\" under Favorites settings.\n> 3. Enable Minimalistic UI under User experience.\n> 4. Enable \"Hide favorites bar initially\" under Minimalistic UI.\n> 5. Check home screen to see there is no favorites bar, even after tapping the screen.\n\nSo the user has:\n- `enable-favorites-bar` = true (Show favorites above search bar)\n- `history-hide` = true (Minimalistic UI)\n- `favorites-hide` = true (Hide favorites bar initially)\n\nIn this scenario, the favorites bar starts hidden (correct). When the user taps the screen, they expect the favorites bar to appear.\n\nNow let's trace what happens:\n\n1. User taps on screen → `ExperienceTweaks.onTouch(event)` → `gd.onTouchEvent(event)` → `onSingleTapUp` or `onSingleTapConfirmed` → `doAction(\"display-history\")`\n\n2. In `doAction(\"display-history\")`:\n   - First, if minimalistic mode is enabled and we're viewing search results with empty query and no adapter items, it runs `HistorySearcher` and shows clear button, hides menu button.\n   - Then, if `isMinimalisticModeEnabledForFavorites()` is true, it shows the favorites bar.\n\n3. The `isMinimalisticModeEnabledForFavorites()` returns true because all three conditions are met.\n\nWait, but there's another piece. Let me look at `updateSearchRecords`:\n\n```java\nvoid updateSearchRecords(boolean isRefresh, String query) {\n    if (query.isEmpty()) {\n        if (isMinimalisticModeEnabled()) {\n            mainActivity.runTask(new NullSearcher(mainActivity));\n            mainEmptyView.setVisibility(View.GONE);\n            if (isMinimalisticModeEnabledForFavorites()) {\n                mainActivity.favoritesBar.setVisibility(View.GONE);\n            }\n        }\n    }\n}\n```\n\nSo when minimalistic mode is enabled and the query is empty, it runs a `NullSearcher` and **hides the favorites bar**.\n\nThen when the user taps:\n1. `doAction(\"display-history\")` is called\n2. It runs `HistorySearcher` which asynchronously populates results\n3. When the searcher finishes, it calls `updateSearchRecords` which would set favorites bar to GONE again\n\nBut wait, the searcher in `doAction(\"display-history\")` only runs when `mainActivity.list.getAdapter() == null || mainActivity.list.getAdapter().isEmpty()`. Let me think about this more carefully.\n\nActually, let me look at what happens at app startup. When the app first launches:\n\n1. `onCreate` is called\n2. `onResume` is called, which sets `mainEmptyView.setVisibility(View.GONE)` and handles minimalistic mode\n3. `updateSearchRecords` is called with an empty query (I need to check where)\n\nLooking at `ForwarderManager.updateSearchRecords`:\n```java\npublic void updateSearchRecords(boolean isRefresh, String query) {\n    if (favoritesForwarder != null) {\n        favoritesForwarder.updateSearchRecords(query);\n    }\n    experienceTweaks.updateSearchRecords(isRefresh, query);\n}\n```\n\nAnd in `ExperienceTweaks.updateSearchRecords`, when query is empty and minimalistic mode is enabled, it runs `NullSearcher` and hides favorites bar.\n\nNow, the key issue: after the user taps, the `doAction(\"display-history\")` method is supposed to show the favorites bar. Let me look at the condition more carefully:\n\n```java\ncase \"display-history\":\n    if (isMinimalisticModeEnabled()) {\n        if (mainActivity.isViewingSearchResults() && mainActivity.searchEditText.getText().toString().isEmpty()) {\n            if (mainActivity.list.getAdapter() == null || mainActivity.list.getAdapter().isEmpty()) {\n                mainActivity.runTask(new HistorySearcher(mainActivity));\n                mainActivity.clearButton.setVisibility(View.VISIBLE);\n                mainActivity.menuButton.setVisibility(View.INVISIBLE);\n            }\n        }\n    }\n\n    if (isMinimalisticModeEnabledForFavorites()) {\n        mainActivity.favoritesBar.setVisibility(View.VISIBLE);\n    }\n    break;\n```\n\nThe favorites bar is shown with `View.VISIBLE` at the end of this case block. So it should be visible after the tap.\n\nBut wait, there's a timing issue! The `mainActivity.runTask(new HistorySearcher(mainActivity))` is async. When the `HistorySearcher` completes, it will call back through `updateSearchRecords` which will hide the favorites bar again:\n\n```java\nvoid updateSearchRecords(boolean isRefresh, String query) {\n    if (query.isEmpty()) {\n        if (isMinimalisticModeEnabled()) {\n            mainActivity.runTask(new NullSearcher(mainActivity));\n            mainEmptyView.setVisibility(View.GONE);\n            if (isMinimalisticModeEnabledForFavorites()) {\n                mainActivity.favoritesBar.setVisibility(View.GONE);  // <-- This hides it again!\n            }\n        }\n    }\n}\n```\n\nBut `isRefresh` is also false for `HistorySearcher`, and in `ForwarderManager`, `updateSearchRecords` is called. Let me check the `Favorites` forwarder too:\n\nActually wait, let me re-read the `updateSearchRecords` in `ExperienceTweaks` more carefully. When `query.isEmpty()` and `isMinimalisticModeEnabled()`:\n- It runs `NullSearcher`\n- Hides `mainEmptyView`\n- Hides favorites bar if minimalistic mode for favorites is enabled\n\nThis happens when the app first loads (in `onResume`). But when the user taps:\n\n1. `doAction(\"display-history\")` is called\n2. If conditions are met, `HistorySearcher` runs (async)\n3. Then `favoritesBar.setVisibility(View.VISIBLE)` is called\n\nNow, what happens after the `HistorySearcher` completes? Let me look at the `Searcher` base class or the `HistorySearcher` to see what callback it makes.\n\nLet me look at the `ForwarderManager` to understand the flow better. I need to find where `updateSearchRecords` is called from the searchers.\n\nActually, looking at the code more carefully, I think the issue is different. Let me trace through step by step:\n\n**At app startup:**\n1. `ExperienceTweaks.onResume()` is called\n2. `isMinimalisticModeEnabled()` is true → `mainEmptyView.setVisibility(View.GONE)`\n3. But `updateSearchRecords` isn't called during `onResume` directly\n\nWait, let me look at `MainActivity` to understand the startup flow better.\n\nActually, I think I need to look at this from a different angle. Let me look at the `Favorites` forwarder and how `updateSearchRecords` is called there.\n\nLooking at the exploration results:\n- In `Favorites.java`, lines 192-199: `updateSearchRecords(String query)` sets favorites bar to VISIBLE when query is empty, GONE when query is not empty.\n\nBut `ExperienceTweaks.updateSearchRecords` sets favorites bar to GONE when minimalistic mode is enabled.\n\nThese are called from `ForwarderManager.updateSearchRecords`. Let me check that.\n\nHmm, I think I need to look at `ForwarderManager` to understand the flow. But based on the exploration results, the `ExperienceTweaks` forwarder handles the minimalistic mode behavior, and it sets the favorites bar to GONE in `updateSearchRecords` when query is empty and minimalistic mode is enabled.\n\nLet me think about the issue differently. The bug report says:\n\n> When enabled, the favorites bar will not appear when I try to tap on the screen.\n\nSo the user has:\n1. `enable-favorites-bar` = true (Show favorites above search bar)\n2. `history-hide` = true (Minimalistic UI)\n3. `favorites-hide` = true (Hide favorites bar initially)\n\nWith this config, the favorites bar should initially be hidden, but when the user taps, it should appear. The code in `doAction(\"display-history\")` does set `favoritesBar.setVisibility(View.VISIBLE)`.\n\nBut here's the issue: in `onSingleTapUp` and `onSingleTapConfirmed`, the `doAction(\"display-history\")` is only called when `history-onclick` is enabled:\n\n```java\nif (prefs.getBoolean(\"history-onclick\", false)) {\n    doAction(\"display-history\");\n}\n```\n\nWait, the user didn't mention `history-onclick`. Maybe that's the issue? But the bug report says \"Previously, I had set up KISS launcher to display my favorites bar when I tapped anywhere on the main screen.\" So the user must have had `history-onclick` enabled to make the favorites bar appear on tap.\n\nHmm, but the bug report says the issue is specifically with \"Hide favorites bar initially\" setting. Let me re-read:\n\n> The cause seems to be the \"Hide favorites bar initially\" setting.\n\nActually, I think the real bug might be something more subtle. Let me look at the code flow again.\n\nWhen minimalistic mode for favorites is enabled (`isMinimalisticModeEnabledForFavorites()` returns true), the flow at startup is:\n\n1. App launches\n2. `onResume()` is called → minimalistic mode is enabled → empty view hidden\n3. At some point, `updateSearchRecords` is called with an empty query (probably from `Favorites` forwarder or from `MainActivity`)\n4. `ExperienceTweaks.updateSearchRecords` hides the favorites bar\n\nNow when the user taps:\n1. `onTouch` → `gd.onTouchEvent` → `onSingleTapUp/Confirmed`\n2. If `history-onclick` is enabled → `doAction(\"display-history\")`\n3. The favorites bar is shown\n\nBut here's the key: what if the `HistorySearcher` that's started inside `doAction(\"display-history\")` completes and calls `updateSearchRecords` which then **hides the favorites bar again**?\n\nLooking at the code:\n```java\ncase \"display-history\":\n    if (isMinimalisticModeEnabled()) {\n        if (mainActivity.isViewingSearchResults() && mainActivity.searchEditText.getText().toString().isEmpty()) {\n            if (mainActivity.list.getAdapter() == null || mainActivity.list.getAdapter().isEmpty()) {\n                mainActivity.runTask(new HistorySearcher(mainActivity));\n                mainActivity.clearButton.setVisibility(View.VISIBLE);\n                mainActivity.menuButton.setVisibility(View.INVISIBLE);\n            }\n        }\n    }\n\n    if (isMinimalisticModeEnabledForFavorites()) {\n        mainActivity.favoritesBar.setVisibility(View.VISIBLE);\n    }\n    break;\n```\n\nThe `HistorySearcher` runs asynchronously. After it finishes, it likely calls some callback that triggers `updateSearchRecords`. And in `ExperienceTweaks.updateSearchRecords`:\n\n```java\nvoid updateSearchRecords(boolean isRefresh, String query) {\n    if (query.isEmpty()) {\n        if (isMinimalisticModeEnabled()) {\n            mainActivity.runTask(new NullSearcher(mainActivity));\n            mainEmptyView.setVisibility(View.GONE);\n            if (isMinimalisticModeEnabledForFavorites()) {\n                mainActivity.favoritesBar.setVisibility(View.GONE);  // <-- HIDES IT!\n            }\n        }\n    }\n}\n```\n\nSo the sequence would be:\n1. User taps → `doAction(\"display-history\")` → favorites bar is shown (VISIBLE)\n2. `HistorySearcher` completes → triggers `updateSearchRecords` → favorites bar is hidden (GONE) again\n\nBut wait, the `HistorySearcher` runs a search with an empty query, which returns history results. The query string would still be empty. So when the searcher completes and calls back, `updateSearchRecords` would be called with `query = \"\"`, and it would hide the favorites bar again.\n\nActually, let me look at how `Searcher` callbacks work. The `Searcher` class likely has a callback mechanism. Let me check.\n\nI should look at the `Searcher` base class and `ForwarderManager` to understand the callback flow. Let me also check `MainActivity` to see how searchers are run and what callbacks they trigger.\n\nActually, I think I need to focus on the `isViewingSearchResults()` check. The key question is: at app startup, what does `isViewingSearchResults()` return? If it returns `false`, then the condition in `doAction(\"display-history\")`:\n\n```java\nif (mainActivity.isViewingSearchResults() && mainActivity.searchEditText.getText().toString().isEmpty()) {\n```\n\n...would not enter the `HistorySearcher` block. But the favorites bar visibility change (`mainActivity.favoritesBar.setVisibility(View.VISIBLE)`) is outside the `isMinimalisticModeEnabled()` block, so it would still execute.\n\nWait, let me re-read the `doAction(\"display-history\")` more carefully:\n\n```java\ncase \"display-history\":\n    // if minimalistic mode is enabled,\n    if (isMinimalisticModeEnabled()) {\n        // and we're currently in minimalistic mode with no results,\n        // and we're not looking at the app list\n        if (mainActivity.isViewingSearchResults() && mainActivity.searchEditText.getText().toString().isEmpty()) {\n            if (mainActivity.list.getAdapter() == null || mainActivity.list.getAdapter().isEmpty()) {\n                mainActivity.runTask(new HistorySearcher(mainActivity));\n                mainActivity.clearButton.setVisibility(View.VISIBLE);\n                mainActivity.menuButton.setVisibility(View.INVISIBLE);\n            }\n        }\n    }\n\n    if (isMinimalisticModeEnabledForFavorites()) {\n        mainActivity.favoritesBar.setVisibility(View.VISIBLE);\n    }\n    break;\n```\n\nThe favorites bar is always shown when `isMinimalisticModeEnabledForFavorites()` is true, regardless of whether we're viewing search results. So the tap handler should show the favorites bar.\n\nHmm, but what if the problem is that `isViewingSearchResults()` returns `false` at startup? In that case, the `mainActivity.displayKissBar(true)` call in the \"display-apps\" case (which is called by the launcher button) would not work properly. But the user is tapping the screen, not pressing the KISS button.\n\nWait, I think I may have been overthinking this. Let me re-read the `onDisplayKissBar` method:\n\n```java\nvoid onDisplayKissBar(boolean display) {\n    if (isMinimalisticModeEnabledForFavorites() && !display) {\n        mainActivity.favoritesBar.setVisibility(View.GONE);\n    }\n    ...\n}\n```\n\nWhen the kiss bar is closed (`display = false`), the favorites bar is hidden. But this should only affect when the kiss bar is explicitly opened and then closed.\n\nOK, I think I need to look at this problem from the `MainActivity` perspective. What happens when the user taps on the screen? Does the touch event reach the gesture detector properly?\n\nActually, let me look at `MainActivity.onTouch`:\n\n```java\n@Override\npublic boolean onTouch(View view, MotionEvent event) {\n    if (forwarderManager.onTouch(view, event)) {\n        return true;\n    }\n    ...\n}\n```\n\nAnd `ForwarderManager.onTouch`:\n\n```java\npublic boolean onTouch(View view, MotionEvent event) {\n    experienceTweaks.onTouch(event);\n    return liveWallpaperForwarder.onTouch(view, event);\n}\n```\n\nSo the touch event is forwarded to `ExperienceTweaks.onTouch(event)`, which calls `gd.onTouchEvent(event)`. This should trigger the gesture detector's `onSingleTapUp` or `onSingleTapConfirmed`.\n\nBut here's a key issue: the `onTouch` listener on `MainActivity` returns `true` if `forwarderManager.onTouch` returns `true`. And `forwarderManager.onTouch` returns whatever `liveWallpaperForwarder.onTouch` returns. The gesture detection result is not propagated.\n\nBut that's probably fine for our purposes.\n\nLet me think about what could go wrong...\n\nActually, I wonder if the issue is simpler than I think. Let me look at the condition in `isMinimalisticModeEnabledForFavorites()`:\n\n```java\nprivate boolean isMinimalisticModeEnabledForFavorites() {\n    return prefs.getBoolean(\"history-hide\", false) && prefs.getBoolean(\"favorites-hide\", false) && prefs.getBoolean(\"enable-favorites-bar\", true);\n}\n```\n\nThis requires:\n1. `history-hide` = true (Minimalistic UI is enabled)\n2. `favorites-hide` = true (Hide favorites bar initially is enabled)\n3. `enable-favorites-bar` = true (Show favorites above search bar is enabled)\n\nThe bug report says all three of these are enabled. So `isMinimalisticModeEnabledForFavorites()` should return true.\n\nNow, looking at the tap handler:\n```java\nif (prefs.getBoolean(\"history-onclick\", false)) {\n    doAction(\"display-history\");\n}\n```\n\nThe user might not have `history-onclick` enabled! If `history-onclick` is disabled, then `doAction(\"display-history\")` is never called, and the favorites bar is never shown.\n\nBut the user says \"Previously, I had set up KISS launcher to display my favorites bar when I tapped anywhere on the main screen.\" This suggests that `history-onclick` was enabled in the past.\n\nHowever, the bug report doesn't explicitly mention `history-onclick`. The user describes:\n1. Set an app as a favorite\n2. Enable \"Show favorites above search bar\"\n3. Enable Minimalistic UI\n4. Enable \"Hide favorites bar initially\"\n5. Check home screen to see there is no favorites bar, even after tapping\n\nSo the reproduction steps don't include enabling `history-onclick`. Maybe that's the issue? Or maybe the bug is that `history-onclick` was enabled but stopped working after some update.\n\nActually, re-reading the bug report title: \"Favorites bar doesn't appear under specific UI settings\". And the description: \"After updating to a newer version of KISS launcher, I am no longer able to make the shortcut bar appear.\"\n\nSo something changed in a newer version that broke this functionality. This is a regression.\n\nLet me think about what could have changed... The `doAction(\"display-history\")` method has a condition that checks `mainActivity.isViewingSearchResults()`. What if this check didn't exist before, or what if it returns `false` when it should return `true`?\n\nActually, wait. Let me look more carefully at the conditions:\n\n```java\ncase \"display-history\":\n    if (isMinimalisticModeEnabled()) {\n        if (mainActivity.isViewingSearchResults() && mainActivity.searchEditText.getText().toString().isEmpty()) {\n            if (mainActivity.list.getAdapter() == null || mainActivity.list.getAdapter().isEmpty()) {\n                mainActivity.runTask(new HistorySearcher(mainActivity));\n                mainActivity.clearButton.setVisibility(View.VISIBLE);\n                mainActivity.menuButton.setVisibility(View.INVISIBLE);\n            }\n        }\n    }\n\n    if (isMinimalisticModeEnabledForFavorites()) {\n        mainActivity.favoritesBar.setVisibility(View.VISIBLE);\n    }\n    break;\n```\n\nThe `HistorySearcher` runs only if we're viewing search results, the query is empty, and the adapter is empty or null. But the favorites bar visibility is set regardless of these conditions.\n\nHmm, but maybe there's a race condition. When the user taps, the favorites bar is set to VISIBLE. But then something else sets it to GONE. What could cause that?\n\nLooking at the `Favorites` forwarder, in `onDataSetChanged()`, the favorites bar is set to VISIBLE:\n```java\n@Override\npublic void onDataSetChanged() {\n    // ...\n    if (query.isEmpty()) {\n        mainActivity.favoritesBar.setVisibility(View.VISIBLE);\n    } else {\n        mainActivity.favoritesBar.setVisibility(View.GONE);\n    }\n}\n```\n\nBut this is the opposite direction.\n\nOK, I think I need to take a step back and think about what the expected behavior should be:\n\n**Expected behavior (per the bug report):**\n- When \"Hide favorites bar initially\" is enabled, the favorites bar should be hidden when the app first launches.\n- When the user taps the screen, the favorites bar should appear.\n\n**Current behavior:**\n- The favorites bar is hidden when the app launches. ✓\n- The favorites bar does NOT appear when the user taps. ✗\n\nThe only place where the favorites bar is shown on tap is in `doAction(\"display-history\")`, which is triggered by the gesture detector when `history-onclick` is enabled. So if `history-onclick` is not enabled, the tap won't show the favorites bar.\n\nBut wait, the user says \"Previously, I had set up KISS launcher to display my favorites bar when I tapped anywhere on the main screen.\" This suggests that `history-onclick` was enabled.\n\nMaybe the bug is that `history-onclick` is now disabled by default? Or maybe the issue is that the favorites bar is immediately hidden again after being shown?\n\nLet me look at `MainActivity` to see if there's something that hides the favorites bar immediately after the tap.\n\nActually, I think the issue might be related to how the `Favorites` forwarder handles the data. Let me look at the `Favorites` forwarder more carefully.\n\nHmm, I've been analyzing the `ExperienceTweaks` code but I should also check the `MainActivity` to understand what `isViewingSearchResults()` returns and how the kiss bar display works.\n\nLet me read the relevant parts of MainActivity.\n\nActually, let me take a different approach. Let me look at what changed in recent commits or if there are any comments in the code that indicate a bug.\n\nWait, looking at the git log from the context: \"e854e91 harness baseline\" — this is a baseline snapshot. So the current code is the buggy version, and I need to figure out what the correct behavior should be.\n\nLet me re-examine the code flow one more time:\n\n1. User enables `history-hide` (minimalistic UI), `favorites-hide` (hide favorites bar initially), and `enable-favorites-bar` (show favorites above search bar).\n\n2. At app startup:\n   - `onResume()` is called → minimalistic mode enabled → empty view hidden\n   - At some point, `updateSearchRecords` is called with an empty query\n   - `ExperienceTweaks.updateSearchRecords` hides the favorites bar\n\n3. User taps the screen:\n   - `onTouch` → `gd.onTouchEvent` → `onSingleTapUp` or `onSingleTapConfirmed`\n   - If `history-onclick` is enabled → `doAction(\"display-history\")`\n   - In `doAction(\"display-history\")`:\n     - If `isMinimalisticModeEnabled()` and we're viewing search results with empty query and empty adapter → `HistorySearcher` runs\n     - If `isMinimalisticModeEnabledForFavorites()` → favorites bar is shown\n\nThe favorites bar SHOULD be shown here. But the user says it doesn't appear. So what's wrong?\n\nLet me check if there's an issue with the condition order. The `doAction(\"display-history\")` method:\n\n```java\ncase \"display-history\":\n    if (isMinimalisticModeEnabled()) {\n        if (mainActivity.isViewingSearchResults() && mainActivity.searchEditText.getText().toString().isEmpty()) {\n            if (mainActivity.list.getAdapter() == null || mainActivity.list.getAdapter().isEmpty()) {\n                mainActivity.runTask(new HistorySearcher(mainActivity));\n                mainActivity.clearButton.setVisibility(View.VISIBLE);\n                mainActivity.menuButton.setVisibility(View.INVISIBLE);\n            }\n        }\n    }\n\n    if (isMinimalisticModeEnabledForFavorites()) {\n        mainActivity.favoritesBar.setVisibility(View.VISIBLE);\n    }\n    break;\n```\n\nThe `mainActivity.favoritesBar.setVisibility(View.VISIBLE)` is on line after the `HistorySearcher` block. This should always execute when `isMinimalisticModeEnabledForFavorites()` is true.\n\nWait, could it be that `isMinimalisticModeEnabledForFavorites()` is returning false even though the user has all three settings enabled? Let me check:\n\n```java\nprivate boolean isMinimalisticModeEnabledForFavorites() {\n    return prefs.getBoolean(\"history-hide\", false) && prefs.getBoolean(\"favorites-hide\", false) && prefs.getBoolean(\"enable-favorites-bar\", true);\n}\n```\n\nThe defaults are:\n- `history-hide` = false\n- `favorites-hide` = false  \n- `enable-favorites-bar` = true\n\nBut the user has enabled all three. So this should return true.\n\nHmm, wait. Let me look at the preferences.xml again:\n- `enable-favorites-bar` default = true\n- `favorites-hide` default = false, depends on `history-hide`\n- `history-hide` default = false\n\nOK, let me look at a different angle. What if the problem is with the `isViewingSearchResults()` check? If this returns false when the user first starts the app, the `HistorySearcher` won't run. But the favorites bar should still be shown because that code is outside the `isViewingSearchResults()` check.\n\nActually, I just realized something. Let me re-read the `onSingleTapUp` and `onSingleTapConfirmed` methods:\n\n```java\n@Override\npublic boolean onSingleTapUp(MotionEvent e) {\n    // Double tap disabled: display history directly\n    if(!prefs.getBoolean(\"double-tap\", false)) {\n        if (prefs.getBoolean(\"history-onclick\", false)) {\n            doAction(\"display-history\");\n        }\n    }\n    return super.onSingleTapUp(e);\n}\n\n@Override\npublic boolean onSingleTapConfirmed(MotionEvent e) {\n    // Double tap enabled: wait to confirm this is indeed a single tap, not a double tap\n    if(prefs.getBoolean(\"double-tap\", false)) {\n        if (prefs.getBoolean(\"history-onclick\", false)) {\n            doAction(\"display-history\");\n        }\n    }\n    return super.onSingleTapConfirmed(e);\n}\n```\n\nThe tap handler calls `doAction(\"display-history\")` only if `history-onclick` is enabled. If `history-onclick` is NOT enabled, nothing happens on tap.\n\nNow, here's the issue I think: **The user is expected to enable `history-onclick` to show the favorites bar on tap.** But the bug report says the user previously could make the favorites bar appear by tapping, which means they must have had `history-onclick` enabled. After the update, something changed.\n\nBut actually, maybe the bug is that `history-onclick` should NOT be a requirement for showing the favorites bar when minimalistic mode + hide favorites bar initially are enabled. The user expects to tap the screen to show the favorites bar, regardless of whether they want to display history on click.\n\nActually, re-reading the bug report more carefully:\n\n> 1. Set an app as a favorite.\n> 2. Enable \"Show favorites above search bar\" under Favorites settings.\n> 3. Enable Minimalistic UI under User experience.\n> 4. Enable \"Hide favorites bar initially\" under Minimalistic UI.\n> 5. Check home screen to see there is no favorites bar, even after tapping the screen.\n\nThe reproduction steps don't mention `history-onclick`. So the user expects the favorites bar to appear on tap WITHOUT needing to enable `history-onclick`.\n\nI think the bug is: when `isMinimalisticModeEnabledForFavorites()` is true (meaning the user has enabled minimalistic UI and hide favorites bar initially), the favorites bar should be shown on tap regardless of the `history-onclick` setting. The `history-onclick` setting is about displaying history on tap, but it shouldn't gate the favorites bar visibility.\n\nSo the fix would be to unconditionally show the favorites bar on tap when `isMinimalisticModeEnabledForFavorites()` is true, regardless of the `history-onclick` setting.\n\nBut wait, that would mean the favorites bar appears on tap even when the user doesn't want history to appear. Let me think about this more carefully.\n\nActually, looking at the bug report again:\n\n> Previously, I had set up KISS launcher to display my favorites bar when I tapped anywhere on the main screen.\n\nThis confirms that the user previously could make the favorites bar appear on tap. So either:\n1. `history-onclick` was enabled and still is (and something else changed)\n2. `history-onclick` was disabled (and still is) and the favorites bar used to appear without it\n\nGiven that the reproduction steps don't mention `history-onclick`, I think the expected behavior is that the favorites bar should appear on tap even when `history-onclick` is disabled, as long as `isMinimalisticModeEnabledForFavorites()` is true.\n\nBut the current code gates the entire `doAction(\"display-history\")` behind `history-onclick`. So if `history-onclick` is disabled, the favorites bar never appears.\n\nHmm, but maybe the fix is simpler. Let me look at the `doAction(\"display-history\")` case more carefully:\n\n```java\ncase \"display-history\":\n    if (isMinimalisticModeEnabled()) {\n        if (mainActivity.isViewingSearchResults() && mainActivity.searchEditText.getText().toString().isEmpty()) {\n            if (mainActivity.list.getAdapter() == null || mainActivity.list.getAdapter().isEmpty()) {\n                mainActivity.runTask(new HistorySearcher(mainActivity));\n                mainActivity.clearButton.setVisibility(View.VISIBLE);\n                mainActivity.menuButton.setVisibility(View.INVISIBLE);\n            }\n        }\n    }\n\n    if (isMinimalisticModeEnabledForFavorites()) {\n        mainActivity.favoritesBar.setVisibility(View.VISIBLE);\n    }\n    break;\n```\n\nThe favorites bar is shown at the end of this case block. The problem is that this code only runs when `history-onclick` is enabled.\n\nI think the fix should be to move the favorites bar visibility logic outside of the `history-onclick` gate. When `isMinimalisticModeEnabledForFavorites()` is true and the user taps, the favorites bar should be shown.\n\nWait, actually, let me look at the problem from a different angle. What if the issue is that the `HistorySearcher` completion hides the favorites bar?\n\nWhen `HistorySearcher` completes, it calls back to the UI thread. What does it do? Let me check the `Searcher` base class and how callbacks work.\n\nActually, I should just look at the `MainActivity` and `ForwarderManager` to understand the