conditional logic inside `doAction`:\n\nWait, but that would change the behavior. If `history-onclick` is disabled, we don't want to run `HistorySearcher` (which is inside the `doAction(\"display-history\")` case). But we DO want to show the favorites bar.\n\nOK, let me think about this differently. The `doAction(\"display-history\")` case does two things:\n1. Runs `HistorySearcher` (conditional on `history-onclick` and other conditions)\n2. Shows the favorites bar (conditional on `isMinimalisticModeEnabledForFavorites()`)\n\nThese should be independent. The `HistorySearcher` part should remain gated by `history-onclick`. The favorites bar part should be unconditional (when minimalistic mode for favorites is enabled).\n\nSo my fix is:\n\n1. In `doAction(\"display-history\")`, keep the `HistorySearcher` part gated by `history-onclick` (move the check inside the case).\n2. Keep the favorites bar part as is.\n3. In the gesture detector's tap handlers, call `doAction(\"display-history\")` unconditionally (remove the `history-onclick` gate).\n\nThis way, `doAction(\"display-history\")` always runs, but the `HistorySearcher` only runs when `history-onclick` is enabled. The favorites bar always shows when `isMinimalisticModeEnabledForFavorites()` is true.\n\nActually wait, let me re-read the `doAction(\"display-history\")` case:\n\n```java\ncase \"display-history\":\n    if (isMinimalisticModeEnabled()) {\n        if (mainActivity.isViewingSearchResults() && mainActivity.searchEditText.getText().toString().isEmpty()) {\n            if (mainActivity.list.getAdapter() == null || mainActivity.list.getAdapter().isEmpty()) {\n                mainActivity.runTask(new HistorySearcher(mainActivity));\n                mainActivity.clearButton.setVisibility(View.VISIBLE);\n                mainActivity.menuButton.setVisibility(View.INVISIBLE);\n            }\n        }\n    }\n\n    if (isMinimalisticModeEnabledForFavorites()) {\n        mainActivity.favoritesBar.setVisibility(View.V